    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\( 1 + 6 \\cdot 2 - 3 + 5 \\cdot 4 \\div 2 \\), we need to follow the order of operations, often remembered by the acronym PEMDAS (Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division (from left to right), Addition and Subtraction (from left to right)).\n\n1. **Multiplication and Division:**\n   - There are two operations involving division and multiplication step 1, respectively.\n\n2. **From left to right operation takes place first, multiplying first:\n   - \\( 6 \\cdot 2 = 12 \\)\n   - \\( 5 \\cdot 4 = 20 \\)\n   - Resulting equation now is \\( 1 + 12 - 3 + 20 \\div 2 \\)\n\n3. **Next is 20 divided by 2**\n   - \\( 20 \\div 2 = 10 \\)\n   - Now, the equation becomes \\( 1 + 12 - 3 + 10 \\)\n\n4. **Finally, the addition and subtraction proceeds at those indices from left to right**\n   - First add 1 and 12:\n     - \\( 1 + 12 = 13 \\)\n   - Then subtract 3:\n     - \\( 13 - 3 = 10 \\)\n   - Lastly, add the remaining 10 to it:\n     - \\( 10 + 10 = 20 \\)\n\nTherefore, the final answer of the expression \\( 1 + 6 \\cdot 2 - 3 + 5 \\cdot 4 \\div 2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).",
